n-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="168">Absolutely! Let’s sketch out how you could prototype PlenumHub using SpherePop calculus, and I’ll outline how to build a SpherePop calculus interpreter as part of that.</p>
<h3 data-start="170" data-end="228">Building a PlenumHub Prototype with SpherePop Calculus</h3>
<h4 data-start="230" data-end="272">1. <strong data-start="238" data-end="272">SpherePop Calculus: The Basics</strong></h4>
<p data-start="274" data-end="570">SpherePop calculus is a playful yet powerful formal system you’ve developed that treats computational processes like popping semantic bubbles, or spheres. Each sphere represents a unit of meaning or a small computational step, and “popping” it means evaluating or transforming it into a new form.</p>
<h4 data-start="572" data-end="618">2. <strong data-start="580" data-end="618">Conceptual Design of the Prototype</strong></h4>
<p data-start="620" data-end="867">To build a PlenumHub prototype, we’ll think of each semantic module as a sphere that can be popped and transformed. The interpreter will handle these spheres, allowing users to merge, evolve, and interact with modules through SpherePop operations.</p>
<h4 data-start="869" data-end="915">3. <strong data-start="877" data-end="915">Building the SpherePop Interpreter</strong></h4>
<p data-start="917" data-end="1013">Let’s outline a simple interpreter in Python (you can extend this to other languages as needed):</p>
<ol data-start="1015" data-end="2377">
<li data-start="1015" data-end="1414">
<p data-start="1018" data-end="1164"><strong data-start="1018" data-end="1049">Define the Sphere Structure</strong>:<br data-start="1050" data-end="1053">
