<h1 data-start="301" data-end="388"><strong data-start="303" data-end="388">PlenumHub and SpherePop Calculus: A Formal Foundation for Semantic Infrastructure</strong></h1>
<h2 data-start="390" data-end="405"><strong data-start="393" data-end="405">Abstract</strong></h2>
<p data-start="407" data-end="1133">Contemporary collaboration systems—social networks, code repositories, and knowledge graphs—lack coherent <em data-start="513" data-end="529">semantic state</em>, <em data-start="531" data-end="554">typed transformations</em>, and <em data-start="560" data-end="597">entropy-respecting composition laws</em>. PlenumHub is introduced as a <strong data-start="628" data-end="660">semantic computing substrate</strong> based on SpherePop calculus, in which knowledge units are <em data-start="719" data-end="758">typed, transformable semantic spheres</em> admitting algebraic composition, formal versioning, modality completion, and rule-based rewriting. We specify the theoretical requirements leading to PlenumHub, formalize SpherePop as a typed rewriting system with categorical semantics, provide a syntax and operational semantics for the SpherePop DSL, and outline reference parsers sufficient to implement the architecture.</p>
<hr data-start="1135" data-end="1138">
<h2 data-start="1140" data-end="1191"><strong data-start="1143" data-end="1191">1. The Need for a Semantic Compute Substrate</strong></h2>
<p data-start="1193" data-end="1265">Existing infrastructures (Git, social feeds, vector DBs, graph DBs) are:</p>
<ol data-start="1267" data-end="1672">
<li data-start="1267" data-end="1332">
<p data-start="1270" data-end="1332"><strong data-start="1270" data-end="1292">Semantically lossy</strong> — meaning is not preserved under merge.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1333" data-end="1418">
<p data-start="1336" data-end="1418"><strong data-start="1336" data-end="1359">Modality-incomplete</strong> — no requirement that content forms a closed media object.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1419" data-end="1501">
<p data-start="1422" data-end="1501"><strong data-start="1422" data-end="1441">Rewrite-unaware</strong> — transformations happen ad-hoc, not as provable morphisms.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1502" data-end="1583">
<p data-start="1505" data-end="1583"><strong data-start="1505" data-end="1526">Entropy-unmodeled</strong> — systems cannot detect when a merge increases disorder.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1584" data-end="1672">
<p data-start="1587" data-end="1672"><strong data-start="1587" data-end="1608">Langauge-external</strong> — change operations are not themselves <em data-start="1648" data-end="1657">objects</em> in the system.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="1674" data-end="1726">A <em data-start="1676" data-end="1704">semantic compute substrate</em> must instead support:</p>
<ul data-start="1728" data-end="2094">
<li data-start="1728" data-end="1807">
<p data-start="1730" data-end="1807"><strong data-start="1730" data-end="1754">Typed semantic units</strong> whose internal state and interfaces are inspectable.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1808" data-end="1904">
<p data-start="1810" data-end="1904"><strong data-start="1810" data-end="1838">First-class rewrite laws</strong> that compose associatively where semantic coherence is preserved.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1905" data-end="1959">
<p data-start="1907" data-end="1959"><strong data-start="1907" data-end="1936">Cross-modality completion</strong> (Media-Quine closure).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1960" data-end="2034">
<p data-start="1962" data-end="2034"><strong data-start="1962" data-end="1998">Entropy bounds on transformation</strong> to ensure merges decrease disorder.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2035" data-end="2094">
<p data-start="2037" data-end="2094"><strong data-start="2037" data-end="2060">Verifiable grammars</strong> describing legal transformations.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2096" data-end="2128">PlenumHub is one such substrate.</p>
<hr data-start="2130" data-end="2133">
<h2 data-start="2135" data-end="2186"><strong data-start="2138" data-end="2186">2. SpherePop Calculus: Objects and Morphisms</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="2188" data-end="2210">2.1 Sphere Objects</h3>
<p data-start="2212" data-end="2247">A <strong data-start="2214" data-end="2224">sphere</strong> is a semantic capsule:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma = (I, T, M, E, S)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">σ</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">I</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.10903em;">M</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">E</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="2281" data-end="2287">where:</p>
