</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Digital collaboration has converged on two dominant paradigms: version control systems optimized for </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{textual diffs and recursion on files} (e.g., Git), and social platforms optimized for </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{chronological feeds and engagement signals}. Neither framework models knowledge as a dynamical, multimodal, entropy-bearing object. Both assume that:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> information is transmitted or edited, not </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{thermodynamically transformed},
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> meaning fidelity is informal and external to the system,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> media domains (text, audio, image, code, math) do not require closure under semantic completion,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and conflict resolution is syntactic, not </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{model-theoretic}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

However, scientific discourse, collective memory, and multimodal reasoning systems exhibit behavior that cannot be captured faithfully in file-diff or feed paradigms. In particular:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Knowledge objects are not files, but field states}
In RSVP-based semantic physics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{flyxion</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>rsvp}, a unit of knowledge behaves more like a field configuration than a static document. Each knowledge object </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> carries:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a scalar content payload per modality (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>.M(k)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>),
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a vector of possible derivations (rule pathways),
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and an entropy signature </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> reflecting internal inconsistency, ambiguity, or underdetermination.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}
Merging or transforming knowledge necessarily creates entropy flux, analogous to Landauer dissipation in physical computation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{landauer}.

Hence, an adequate knowledge geometry must support </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{entropy-aware transforms} rather than assuming lossless diffs.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Meaning completeness requires media closure (the Media-Quine condition)}
Human communication expects semantic equivalence across modalities: a theorem should admit a narrative explanation; a text should yield speech; a diagram should admit description; recorded speech should admit transcription. This motivates the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Media-Quine closure condition}:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
Q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Leftrightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{required}}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>.M(k) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

That is, a knowledge object is complete only if it can regenerate itself across modality boundaries, filling gaps using lawful transducers rather than human guesswork. This is not a convenience feature but a consistency condition of the semantic substrate.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Unregulated combination leads to semantic heat death}
Naive merging of divergent narratives accumulates contradictions. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be two knowledge objects. Their merge </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> must satisfy:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>(E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a), E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b)) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{merge}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Without this constraint, iterated merges diverge toward maximal entropy (total incoherence), exactly as unregulated gradient accumulation destabilizes learning systems </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{bengio</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>dl}. Thus, knowledge aggregation must be </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{entropy-regularized}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Provenance is not history, but type-respecting rule homotopy}
Instead of linear commit logs, semantic evolution forms a homotopy class of rule paths:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Different derivation paths may converge to extensionally equal knowledge objects, forming equivalence classes of proofs, paraphrases, compilations, or rewrites. This demands a rule-level type system:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{type}} r : a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> b, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{chain}} r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1;r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>;r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n : a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> b
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Without type discipline, chains of multimodal transformations fail unpredictably. With it, knowledge evolution becomes referentially compositional, not merely chronologically accumulated.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{The system we require}
From these constraints, we derive the design requirements of the PlenumHub computational substrate:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{table}[H]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\centering</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tabular}{l l}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\toprule</span></span><span>
Property </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Requirement </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\midrule</span></span><span>
Semantic unit </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Multimodal spheres </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> = (I,T,M,E,S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, not files or posts </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Consistency </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Entropy-regulated merge operations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Completeness </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Media-Quine closure </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Evolution </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Typed rule chains, not text patches </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Equivalence </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Homotopy classes of derivations, not linear history </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Verification </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Static chain type checking and entropy bounds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Conflict resolution </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Semantic mediation, not last-write-wins </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bottomrule</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tabular}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\caption</span></span><span>{Requirements motivating the PlenumHub formal system.}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{table}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Connection to the rest of this work}
This paper introduces a computational realization of these principles by:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Defining a typed rule calculus for multimodal knowledge,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Specifying entropy-constrained merge semantics,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Formalizing Media-Quine closure as a semantic invariant,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Providing executable reduction rules (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\S</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:reduction}),
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> And presenting a reference interpreter design (Appendix~A).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

This system does not replace version control or social interaction; it </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{replaces their underlying data model} with one based on semantic validity, not syntactic difference or engagement dynamics. In doing so, it creates a foundation on which collaborative knowledge systems, theorem archives, multimodal research objects, and generative reasoning graphs can evolve without semantic collapse.

We now proceed to the formal specification.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
