.6348 11.666L16.6914 11.5361C16.7428 11.3924 16.7797 11.2026 16.8027 10.9209C16.8341 10.5374 16.835 10.0439 16.835 9.33301V6.5C16.835 5.78896 16.8341 5.29563 16.8027 4.91211C16.7797 4.63025 16.7428 4.44063 16.6914 4.29688L16.6348 4.16699C16.4807 3.86474 16.2466 3.61186 15.959 3.43555L15.833 3.36524C15.675 3.28473 15.4636 3.22797 15.0879 3.19727C14.7044 3.16593 14.211 3.16504 13.5 3.16504H10.667C9.9561 3.16504 9.46259 3.16595 9.0791 3.19727C8.79739 3.22028 8.6076 3.2572 8.46387 3.30859L8.33399 3.36524C8.03176 3.51923 7.77886 3.75343 7.60254 4.04102L7.53125 4.16699C7.4508 4.32498 7.39397 4.53655 7.36328 4.91211C7.33985 5.19893 7.33562 5.54719 7.33399 6.00195H9.33301C10.022 6.00195 10.5791 6.00131 11.0293 6.03809C11.4873 6.07551 11.8937 6.15471 12.2705 6.34668L12.4883 6.46875C12.984 6.7728 13.3878 7.20854 13.6533 7.72949L13.7197 7.87207C13.8642 8.20859 13.9292 8.56974 13.9619 8.9707C13.9987 9.42092 13.998 9.97799 13.998 10.667V12.665ZM18.165 9.33301C18.165 10.022 18.1657 10.5791 18.1289 11.0293C18.0961 11.4302 18.0311 11.7914 17.8867 12.1279L17.8203 12.2705C17.5549 12.7914 17.1509 13.2272 16.6553 13.5313L16.4365 13.6533C16.0599 13.8452 15.6541 13.9245 15.1963 13.9619C14.8593 13.9895 14.4624 13.9935 13.9951 13.9951C13.9935 14.4624 13.9895 14.8593 13.9619 15.1963C13.9292 15.597 13.864 15.9576 13.7197 16.2939L13.6533 16.4365C13.3878 16.9576 12.9841 17.3941 12.4883 17.6982L12.2705 17.8203C11.8937 18.0123 11.4873 18.0915 11.0293 18.1289C10.5791 18.1657 10.022 18.165 9.33301 18.165H6.5C5.81091 18.165 5.25395 18.1657 4.80371 18.1289C4.40306 18.0962 4.04235 18.031 3.70606 17.8867L3.56348 17.8203C3.04244 17.5548 2.60585 17.151 2.30176 16.6553L2.17969 16.4365C1.98788 16.0599 1.90851 15.6541 1.87109 15.1963C1.83431 14.746 1.83496 14.1891 1.83496 13.5V10.667C1.83496 9.978 1.83432 9.42091 1.87109 8.9707C1.90851 8.5127 1.98772 8.10625 2.17969 7.72949L2.30176 7.51172C2.60586 7.0159 3.04236 6.6122 3.56348 6.34668L3.70606 6.28027C4.04237 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Platonic Structure, Factored Representations, and the Case for a Semantic Plenum}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:platonic-foundations}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Any collective knowledge system---whether social, scientific, or algorithmic---presupposes that the world admits structured regularities which can be efficiently represented, shared, and transformed. Kumar formalizes this premise as the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Platonic Intelligence Hypothesis}: intelligence requires representations that inherit the symmetries and factorization structure of the world rather than fitting input--output behavior alone </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{akarsh2023platonic}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Symmetry structure of the world}

Let the world be a measurable space </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{W}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> equipped with a transformation group </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> encoding its latent symmetries (e.g., spatial translations, conceptual invariances, logical equivalences). A representation map </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{W}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{well-aligned to reality} when it is equivariant to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> x) = g' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> G,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{W}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Such structure-respecting representations approximate what Plato characterized as the eternal regularities behind the sensory world, and form the substrate for compositional intelligence rather than memorized correlation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Why gradient descent gives the wrong kind of representation}

Standard deep networks learn parameters by minimizing:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>* = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\arg</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{(x,y)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> D}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>[ L(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x), y) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

But functional equivalence does not guarantee representational equivalence. There frequently exist parameters </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1}(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2}(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{but} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1}(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\not</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2}(x)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This demonstrates that SGD discovers solutions that solve the task but do not recover the latent factors or symmetry structure of the world. Such solutions are </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{entangled, unfactored, and non-compositional}, which may suffice for prediction but fail for structured understanding or knowledge interchange.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Evolution finds factors that learning misses}

In contrast, open-ended evolutionary search can discover modular, semantically disentangled representations. Given a population of neural architectures </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, selection </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and mutation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, evolution proceeds:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1} = M(S(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{random}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Empirically, this discovers decompositions of phenotype space:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
V = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2, ..., v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> controls a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{semantically interpretable factor} (jaw width, wing size, curvature, gait, etc.). This induces a modular parameterization:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{mod}}(x) = g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1}), h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2}), ..., h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

with each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> operating on an independent subspace </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> composing the factors.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Adaptability is stronger than generalization}

Kumar further shows that robustness comes not from fitting a static data distribution, but adapting to many related environments </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, ..., E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>* = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\arg</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{j=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{m} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{(x,y)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>[ L(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x), y) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

A truly adaptable representation minimizes loss even on unseen environments:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{(x,y)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m+1}}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>[L(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>*}(x),y)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{low}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This requires latent factors, modularity, and reconfigurability—not memorization.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Curriculum, compositional buildup, and modular induction}

Instead of learning all structure at once, representations assemble through staged exposure:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i+1} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Update}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i, D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i+1}})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

with knowledge composing additively:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{k-1}} + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> encodes a new irreducible regularity. This resembles scientific concept formation, where abstraction layers are stacked rather than learned simultaneously.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Simplicity regularization is insufficient}

While deep learning favors small weights:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>* = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\arg</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[L(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x),y)] + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\noindent</span></span><span> this does </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{not} enforce modularity. A better criterion is independence between learned factors:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
J(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ne</span></span><span> j} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{MI}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}), h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

A useful representation minimizes cross-factor mutual information, yielding disentanglement and interpretability.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Implication: knowledge systems must be factored and symmetry-respecting}

If intelligence depends on discovering Platonic factors, then a collective knowledge substrate should:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> store knowledge in a form that preserves latent factors, not flat text,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> support compositional recombination of independent conceptual modules,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> enforce transformation equivariance (symmetry preservation),
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> penalize representational entropy growth during merges or edits,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> evolve representations by curriculum rather than treating all content uniformly,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and expose modular substructures as first-class citizens rather than hidden embeddings.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

No current social or version-control substrate enforces these constraints. Git tracks diffs, not invariants; embeddings encode similarity, not factored structure; feeds optimize attention, not compositional alignment. This motivates a new kind of knowledge space—one that treats semantic objects as structured fields evolving under lawful transformations.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{This leads directly to the requirements for a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Semantic Plenum}:}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> knowledge objects with typed multimodal components,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> rule-chains as lawful symmetry-preserving operators,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> entropy-constrained merging to prevent semantic heat death,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> media completeness enforced by a quine-closure condition,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and evaluation by structural stability rather than popularity or recency.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

We now formulate the system that realizes these principles.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
