</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

The preceding section established that robust intelligence depends on recovering factored, symmetry-respecting representations rather than fitting input–output correlations. To build a knowledge substrate capable of the same feat, we must now reinterpret these principles in the language of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{semantic dynamics}: the evolution, transformation, and conservation of structured meaning.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Knowledge as a physical quantity, not a container}

Most collaborative systems treat knowledge as a container-like artifact—files, posts, commits, or message payloads. In contrast, symmetry-aware learning implies that a unit of knowledge </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is better modeled as a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{state in a semantic field}, with internal degrees of freedom, conserved structure, and measurable disorder. We therefore describe each knowledge object as:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> = (I, T, M, E, S)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>I</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a unique identity,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a type signature declaring required semantic modalities,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> maps modality keys </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (e.g., text, code, audio, proof, example) to values,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{semantic entropy} of the object,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is its provenance topology (the rule-chain history that generated it).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Unlike document models, the proposal here treats meaning as something with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{internal energy and transformation cost}, not merely external reference.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Transformations as symmetry-preserving operators}

Following the Platonic requirement that representations respect world symmetries, all valid knowledge transforms must be equivariant under meaning-preserving transformations. A rule </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is valid only if it respects the type and covariance structure of the object it modifies:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
r: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{and} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Symmetry}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>') </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Symmetry}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Operationally, this prohibits transforms that destroy latent structure (e.g., paraphrase rules that erase definitions, summaries that discard invariants, or conversions that lose dimensional information).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Entropy as a first-class bound on semantic stability}

Repeated transformation, rewriting, and merging of knowledge behaves analogously to thermodynamic systems: disorder accumulates unless constrained. We define a system-wide invariant:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{valid only if} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span>
E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>(E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a), E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b)) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{merge}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This ensures that new composite knowledge objects do not exceed the entropy budget of their constituents except by a controlled tolerance. Without this constraint, collaborative systems exhibit unbounded semantic drift—what we term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{semantic heat death}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Closure under modality: the Media-Quine principle}

Symmetry in meaning includes not only invariances within a modality but equivalences across them. A concept is incomplete if it cannot regenerate itself in all required semantic embeddings. We therefore define the Media-Quine operator:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
Q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\iff</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{required}},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>.M(k) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

A knowledge object that satisfies </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{semantically closed}: it can render itself across all target modalities (e.g., text </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leftrightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> speech </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leftrightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> symbolic form </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leftrightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> executable logic) without external supplementation. Completion under </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is idempotent, ensuring stability of closure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Why the classical substrates fail}

| Substrate | What it tracks | What it fails to guarantee |
|---|---|---|
| Git | syntactic diffs | semantic invariants, modality closure, entropy growth bounds |
| Social feeds | attention flows | compositional validity, provenance topology, symmetry preservation |
| Vector embeddings | statistical similarity | factor independence, rule-governed transforms, modular structure |
| LLM memory | local persistence | global consistency, typed rule application, semantic entropy control |

None of these systems impose structure at the level required for compositional, equivariant, evolvable meaning.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Toward a semantic substrate with physical law}

To support intelligence that is:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{factor-aware} rather than entangled,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{equivariant} rather than correlation-seeking,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{composable} rather than sequential,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{entropy-bounded} rather than diffusing toward incoherence,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{modality-closed} rather than representationally fragmented,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}
we require a substrate governed not by flows of text or attention, but by </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{laws of semantic physics}.

The following sections formalize these laws as:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a typed calculus for semantic transformations,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a rule system for proof-of-validity in knowledge composition,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and an operational semantics guaranteeing entropy-bounded evolution.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

We now proceed to define this formal system.
