01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{The Typed Semantic Calculus}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:typed-calculus}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Having established that intelligence and collective knowledge both require factored, symmetry-respecting, entropy-bounded representations, we now introduce the formal language for transforming such objects. This section defines the core model: a typed calculus of semantic spheres, rewrite rules, valid compositions, and entropy-constrained reductions. Unlike lambda calculi that model functions or category-theoretic languages that model morphisms, this system models </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{meaning-preserving transformations on multimodal knowledge objects}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Semantic spheres as typed states}

A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{semantic sphere} is the fundamental state object of the system:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> := (I,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> T,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> M,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> E,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> S)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

with components:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>I</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> — unique identity (immutable),
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> — type signature defining required modalities,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> — a finite map from modality keys (text, audio, code, embedding, proof, etc.) to content values,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> — semantic entropy, measuring structural disorder or representational ambiguity,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> — provenance structure (a directed acyclic graph of rule applications generating this sphere).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

A sphere is considered </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{well-typed} when it satisfies its signature:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{valid} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\iff</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> T,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> M(k) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\noindent</span></span><span> This ensures that all required semantic facets are populated, though not necessarily minimal or disentangled.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vspace</span></span><span>{6pt}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Typed transformation rules}

A rule is a typed operator that consumes one modality and produces another (or transforms it internally):

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
r : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Rules must also declare an entropy budget </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> representing the maximum disorder increase they may introduce.

A rule application generates a new sphere:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\noindent</span></span><span> which is valid only if:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>') </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>r
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\noindent</span></span><span> and the modality types match the rule signature:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
M(a) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{and} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> M'(b) = r(M(a))
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Composition of rule chains}

Rules compose sequentially to form semantic programs:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
R = r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1; r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>; r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

A rule chain is well-typed when:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 : a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 : a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n : a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n-1} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

which we summarize as:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> R : a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Execution applies rules in sequence:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

We denote the big-step judgment:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Downarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>R </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

to mean that rule chain </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> successfully transforms </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> into </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> without violating typing or entropy constraints.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vspace</span></span><span>{6pt}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Entropy-controlled semantic merging}

When two spheres interact, they may undergo a merge:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Unlike text diffs or vector blending, semantic merges must satisfy an entropy bound:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>(E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a), E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b)) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{merge}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

If this condition fails, the system must either:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> reject the merge,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> or attempt a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{mediated reconciliation} through intermediate rewrite chains </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a, R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Downarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a , </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Downarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b , </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span>
E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>(E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a), E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b)) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{merge}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vspace</span></span><span>{6pt}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Media-Quine closure operator}

To enforce cross-modal completeness, we define the closure operator:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
Q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{where} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{valid} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\wedge</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> M'(k) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> T
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is idempotent:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
Q(Q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)) = Q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Operationally, this means missing modalities may be synthesized (via learned or symbolic transducers), but once closed, the sphere remains stable under further closure attempts.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vspace</span></span><span>{6pt}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Equivariance and semantic invariants}

A valid transformation must preserve Platonic structure. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the symmetry group acting on meaning-preserving transformations (analogies, refactorings, paraphrases, basis changes in vector encodings, proof re-orderings, etc.). A rule </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is admissible only if it is equivariant under </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
r(g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) = g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> r(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> G
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This ensures that transformations respect invariants rather than accidentally breaking latent structure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vspace</span></span><span>{6pt}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Factor independence constraint}

To avoid entangled, SGD-style representations where internal components are inseparable, spheres may optionally satisfy a modularity constraint:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ne</span></span><span> j} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{MI}(M(k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i), M(k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j)) &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{MI}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denotes mutual information and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> a strict independence threshold. This promotes factored, interpretable knowledge components analogous to disentangled latent variables in machine learning </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{higgins2018beta, tishby2015info}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vspace</span></span><span>{6pt}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Summary of calculus properties}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{table}[H]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\centering</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tabular}{l l}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\toprule</span></span><span>
Property </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Guarantee </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\midrule</span></span><span>
Type safety </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Rules cannot be applied to unsupported modalities </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Entropy boundedness </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> No merge or transform may exceed disorder budget </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Media closure </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Objects become stable under completion </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Equivariance </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Transformations respect symmetry group </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Provenance preservation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> All rewrites extend </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> without overwriting it </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Factor independence </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Optional MI constraint prevents entanglement </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bottomrule</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tabular}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\caption</span></span><span>{Core guarantees of the Semantic Calculus.}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{table}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vspace</span></span><span>{6pt}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Toward an executable semantics}

This calculus is not merely descriptive: it can be directly compiled into an interpreter, where:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> states are sphere records,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> transformations are typed effectful operators,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> composition is rule chaining,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> consistency is enforced by entropy and symmetry checks,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and completion is achieved by Media-Quine closure.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

The reduction semantics and reference execution model implementing this calculus are detailed in Appendix~A.
