</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{SpherePOP Calculus: A Topological Language for Meaning Composition}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:spherepop}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

SpherePOP is a minimal computational calculus for constructing and transforming semantic spheres through adjacency, popping, and topological merge. Unlike symbolic languages that treat expressions as trees or instruction sequences, SpherePOP treats meaning as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{volumes of influence} that interact, exchange content, and fuse according to algebraic and entropic validity rules.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Primitives}

The language operates over the following primitives:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{sphere(id, T, M)} — construct a new sphere with type signature </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and content map </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{pop(A, B, R)} — apply rule-chain </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> from sphere </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> into sphere </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{merge(A, B)} — compute an entropy-constrained fusion of spheres
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{close(A)} — apply Media-Quine closure </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q(A)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

A SpherePOP program is a directed multigraph of sphere transformations:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P} = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where nodes </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are spheres, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the rule vocabulary, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are valid typed rule applications.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Typing rules}

SpherePOP adopts the semantic typing discipline defined in Section~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:typed-calculus}. The central typing judgments are:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> R: a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> b </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>A(a) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span>}
     {</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{pop}(A,B,R) : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{valid}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{E(A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span> B) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>(E(A), E(B)) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{merge}}}
     {</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{merge}(A,B) : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{valid}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>A,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>A(k) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span>}
     {</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{close}(A) = A}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Operational semantics}

SpherePOP uses a small-step reduction system for local rewrites:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(A, B, R) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\longrightarrow</span></span><span> (A, B')
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{where} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span>
B' = R(A) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ and } E(B') </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> E(B)+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>R
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Merge is defined as a guarded rewrite:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(A, B) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\longrightarrow</span></span><span> C
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{where} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span>
C = A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span> B
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

only if entropy bounds and type compatibility are satisfied.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Algebraic laws}

SpherePOP respects the following core identities:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{align*}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Idempotence} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{merge}(A, A) = A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Closure stability} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{close}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{close}(A)) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{close}(A) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Entropy monotonicity} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>: E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{pop}(A, B, R)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> E(B) - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>R </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Type safety} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> P : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{valid} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{no malformed modality accesses}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{align*}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Disentanglement via geometric adjacency}

Spatial adjacency in SpherePOP carries semantic pressure for factorization. Two spheres </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>A,B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> that repeatedly exchange rule-flow are encouraged toward mutual information minimization:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{AdjFactor}(A,B) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{MI}(A,B) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

analogous to disentanglement objectives in representation learning </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{higgins2018beta}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Execution sketch}

A minimal evaluator for SpherePOP reduces a queue of sphere-interaction events:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{verbatim}<span class="hljs-string">
while queue not empty:
    event = pop()
    if typecheck(event) and entropy_ok(event):
        apply(event)
    else:
        mediate(event)
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{verbatim}

SpherePOP is thus </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{not} a functional language or a stack machine, but a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{reactive semantic plasma} where meaning propagates through lawful contact.

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
