01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Cognitive Alignment and Interpretability}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:cog-align}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

A semantic system may be sound, secure, entropy-bounded, and operationally complete while still being </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{cognitively misaligned}: producing knowledge structures that satisfy formal invariants but not human sensemaking, conceptual transparency, or cooperative reasoning. In this section we formalize the conditions under which a Semantic Plenum is not only correct, but </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{aligned to interpretable thought} and resistant to opaque, entangled, or epistemically alien modes of reasoning.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Cognitive alignment as symmetry between agents and representations}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

Alignment requires that the invariances preserved by the system overlap with the invariances meaningful to its users. Let:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{world}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> — the group of real-world semantic symmetries (analogies, reference-preserving substitutions, structural equivalence),
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{system}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> — the group of transformations the calculus treats as meaning-preserving,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{cognitive}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> — the group of transformations humans treat as preserving identity of a concept.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

A necessary condition for cognitive alignment is:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{system}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{cognitive}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{world}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{cognitive}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

In words: the calculus must preserve at least those invariances that humans rely upon for stable meaning. When this overlap shrinks, systems become formally correct but conceptually alien.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Interpretability through factor exposure}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

Following the disentanglement imperative </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{higgins2018beta, tishby2015info}, interpretable semantic states are those whose factorization exposes independent mechanisms rather than high-dimensional entanglements. For a sphere with modality components </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M(k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>, M(k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, interpretability increases as:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) = 1 - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{1}{n(n-1)} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ne</span></span><span> j} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{MI}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(M(k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i), M(k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

A maximally interpretable sphere approaches </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)=1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, at which point modalities share minimal mutual information except where explicitly bound by rule provenance.

Interpretability incentives are therefore implemented as admissible entropy-neutral rewrites that increase </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> even when semantic content is unchanged.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Reasoning transparency through rule provenance}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

Transparency does not arise from inspecting static state, but from reconstructing the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{derivational topology} that produced it. Each sphere carries a proof-shaped lineage graph:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
S = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

We define a trajectory as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{cognitively legible} when:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> i,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{natural-language explanation } </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span>(r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i): r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mapsto</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{communicable rationale}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

That is, every transformation step admits a compact justification in the same modality humans use to explain reasoning. Rule operators may therefore register optional explanation adapters:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
r : a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> b </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightsquigarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> (r,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>r)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

which propagate along </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> into human-readable chains of reasoning, not mere procedural traces.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Detecting and penalizing epistemic opacity}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

Opaque derivations are syntactically valid chains that resist human factorization or natural-language justification. We quantify opacity as:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{O}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) = 1 - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{1}{|S|} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{r </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> S} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{explanation exists for } r</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{O}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)=0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> means full explainability of all steps, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{O}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)=1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> means fully opaque provenance.

Runtime policy may enforce:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Reject}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{if} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{O}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) &gt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{opacity}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

or route high-opacity transitions into a mandatory interpretability mediation queue.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Alignment pressure from curriculum geometry}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

The system’s curriculum scheduler (Section~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:bootstrapping}) is further constrained to favor transformations that satisfy:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I} &gt; 0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\wedge</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{O} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> 0
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Meaning: progression should increase interpretability and must not increase opacity. This couples cognitive alignment to the same evolutionary pressures that govern entropy and validity.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Semantic steering vectors and human decipherability}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

A sphere </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> supports a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{semantic steering vector} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> when a small, interpretable transformation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> induces a predictable semantic shift without increasing entropy:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>' = v(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{such that} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>') </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{and} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>') - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Steerability is a practical criterion for controllability and aligns the calculus with findings in mechanistic interpretability </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{elhage2021circuits}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Summary: Alignment invariants}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

A cognitively aligned Semantic Plenum satisfies the following invariants:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{center}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tabular}{ll}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\toprule</span></span><span>
Invariant </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Meaning </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\midrule</span></span><span>
Symmetry compatibility </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> System invariances overlap human invariances </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Factor visibility </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Modalities expose low mutual information structure </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Rule legibility </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Every rule admits a natural-language rationale </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Opacity bounds </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> No accepted sphere exceeds </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{opacity}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Interpretability growth </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Curriculum increases </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and decreases </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{O}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Steerability </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Small edits produce predictable semantic shifts </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bottomrule</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tabular}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{center}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Conclusion}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

Cognitive alignment is not layered on top of the Semantic Plenum as a moderation heuristic or interpretability add-on, but is instead an </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{intrinsic property of its formal constraints}. Because meaning is manipulated only through typed, entropy-bound, symmetry-respecting, explanation-bearing transformations, the system never evolves representations that are correct but cognitively opaque. Alignment, interpretability, and semantic stability are therefore not outcomes but invariants of execution.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
