</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\title</span></span><span>{Semantic Infrastructure: A Typed, Entropy-Bounded Calculus for Collective Intelligence}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\author</span></span><span>{}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\date</span></span><span>{}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{document}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\maketitle</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{abstract}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
Contemporary knowledge systems—social networks, version control, and machine learning repositories—optimize for exchange or prediction, but not for the structured evolution of meaning. Recent theoretical work argues that intelligence, artificial or collective, depends on recovering the latent symmetries and factorization structure of the world rather than fitting input–output correlations. Unified factored representation theory shows that gradient descent alone does not reliably discover these regularities, while open-ended evolutionary and curriculum-driven processes do, producing modular, interpretable, and transferable representations.

We argue that collaborative knowledge systems must similarly treat ideas as structured objects governed by symmetry, factorization, and entropy, rather than as sequential edits or embedding vectors. To this end, we introduce the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Semantic Plenum} model, in which knowledge objects are multimodal spheres transformed by typed rule morphisms, composed through entropy-constrained merges, and completed under a Media–Quine closure principle ensuring cross-modal completeness.

This paper formalizes the mathematical requirements for such systems, establishes the operational semantics of rule composition and semantic merging, and situates these mechanisms within a thermodynamic theory of representational entropy. The result is a computable substrate for collaborative intelligence that favors structural coherence over syntactic convenience, and semantic stability over unregulated accumulation.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Platonic Structure, Factored Representations, and the Case for a Semantic Plenum}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:platonic-foundations}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Intelligence depends on structured internal representations that align with the symmetries of the world. Let a world model </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{W}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> have transformation group </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> capturing its invariances. A representation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{W}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is well-structured if it is equivariant:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> x) = g' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> G,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{W}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Gradient-descent training minimizes loss but not representational structure:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>* = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\arg</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[L(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x),y)]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

and equivalent predictors may have incompatible internal factorizations:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1}(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2}(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{but}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1}(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\not</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2}(x)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Open-ended evolutionary systems recover modular factors:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{mod}}(x) = g(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1}),</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k}))
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Adaptability further requires optimization across environments:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>*=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\arg</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{(x,y)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}}[L(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x),y)]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

And compositional concept growth follows:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{k-1}} + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Simplicity priors fail to enforce modularity; independence is better measured by factor mutual information:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
J(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ne</span></span><span> j}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{MI}(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}),h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}))
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

A knowledge system must preserve structure, not merely store text. This motivates a typed semantic substrate.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{From Platonic Factors to Semantic Physics}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:semantic-physics}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Knowledge becomes a semantic state:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> = (I,T,M,E,S)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>I</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is identity, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> a modality signature, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> content map, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> entropy, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> transformation provenance. All knowledge evolution is bounded by entropy budgets and symmetry-preserving transforms:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>') </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>r
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Cross-modal completion is enforced by Media–Quine closure:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
Q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Leftrightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> T, M(k)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{The Typed Semantic Calculus}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:typed-calculus}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Typed rule operators:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
r:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> r:A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> B
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Rule chains compose:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1;r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>;r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n : A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> Z
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Entropy-bounded merge:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>(E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a),E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b))+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{merge}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{SpherePOP Calculus}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:spherepop}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Primitive operations:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{sphere}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{pop}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{merge}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{close}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Reductions:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(A,B,R)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span>(A,B') </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{where } B'=R(A)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Key identities:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{merge}(A,A)=A,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{close(close}(A))=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{close}(A)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{PlenumHub Execution Model}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:plenumhub}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Global semantic field:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Nodes broadcast proofs, not content:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Verify}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{valid signature} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\wedge</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{typed} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\wedge</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{entropy-safe}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Interpreter and Runtime Algorithms}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:interpreter}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Main interpreter loop:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{lstlisting}
while Q not empty:
    e = dequeue(Q)
    if not WellTyped(e): continue
    if not EntropyAdmissible(e): continue
    result = Reduce(e)
    if result.success:
        Phi.add(result.sphere)
        P.cache(result.proof)
        Broadcast(result.proof)
    else:
        Mediate(e)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{lstlisting}

Entropy bounds are enforced at every reduction.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Bootstrapping, UX, and Developer Tooling}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:bootstrapping}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Foundational spheres initialize the universe:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{types}},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{rules}},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{identity}},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{closure}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

User actions are SpherePOP reductions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Security, Adversarial Stability, and Semantic Attacks}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:security}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Entropy attack bound for any transformation chain:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0)+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Adversarial merges are rejected unless mediated within bounds.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Proof Theory}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:proof-theory}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Core guarantees:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{center}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tabular}{ll}
Type Safety </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> never ill-typed </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Progress </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> never stuck </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Entropy Bounded </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> no unbudgeted drift </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tabular}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{center}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Cognitive Alignment and Interpretability}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:cog-align}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Interpretability:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)=1-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{1}{n(n-1)}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ne</span></span><span> j}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{MI}(M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i,M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Opacity bound:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{O}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{opacity}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Synthesis and Future Directions}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

The Semantic Plenum defines a thermodynamically-constrained knowledge runtime where ideas evolve via typed, entropy-bounded, symmetry-aware transformations rather than unbounded textual drift. Future work includes empirical benchmarks, scalable mediation, steering vectors, and cross-agent alignment.

