</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Category-Theoretic Foundation of PlenumHub}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:cat-foundations}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

To ensure that knowledge evolution in PlenumHub composes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{lawfully} rather than heuristically, we ground SpherePOP in category theory. This section establishes that semantic transformations form a well-behaved monoidal category, that merge constructs a partial symmetric monoid, and that Media-Quine closure is a reflective subcategory via an adjunction.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{The Category of Semantic Spheres}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Sphere Category </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the category where:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Objects are well-typed semantic spheres
          </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> = (I, T, M, E, S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfying
          </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M(k)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for all </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Morphisms are </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{typed rule chains}
          </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that
          </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Downarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>R </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> without violating type or entropy budgets.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Identity morphisms </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{id}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are empty rule chains.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Composition is rule-chain concatenation:
          </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 := R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1;R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Category Laws]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfies associativity and identity:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> (R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1) = (R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{id}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> R = R = R </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{id}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Associativity follows immediately from associativity of list concatenation.
Identity holds since concatenating an empty chain performs no reductions and introduces no entropy or type effects.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Symmetric Monoidal Structure}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

Sphere transformations do not occur in isolation: multiple spheres interact, transform in parallel, and merge. This behavior is captured by a monoidal product.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Tensor on Spheres]
Define a bifunctor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span>:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> by:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b :=
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(
    I</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ab},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cup</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cup</span></span><span> M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>(E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a,E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b),</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqcup</span></span><span> S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqcup</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is disjoint union of provenance DAGs with tagged namespaces.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Tensor on Morphisms]
Given </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, define:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b := R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\parallel</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
as parallel rule execution on disjoint modality domains.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span>,I</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a Symmetric Monoidal Category]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>I</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bot</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span>,0,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the empty sphere. Then:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a bifunctor,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is associative up to isomorphism,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>I</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a two-sided unit up to isomorphism,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and a symmetry isomorphism </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> exists.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Bifunctoriality follows from independent rule execution and disjoint effects.
Associativity holds because </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cup</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqcup</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are associative up to bijection relabeling.
The unit laws follow since union with the empty sphere preserves modalities, entropy, and provenance.
Symmetry is given by the natural swap bijection on both modality maps and provenance tags.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Interpretation.}
This result formalizes that:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> semantic transformations compose without evaluation order ambiguity,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> separate sphere evolutions commute when non-interfering,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> knowledge can be partitioned, transformed separately, and recombined soundly.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Merge as a Partial Commutative Monoid}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

Merging is not total: it exists only when entropy growth is bounded.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Entropy-Gated Merge]
Define a partial operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span> : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cup</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b =
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{cases}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{if } E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>(E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a,E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b)+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{merge}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{otherwise.}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{cases}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Partial Symmetric Monoid]
When defined, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> commutative: </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> idempotent: </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and associative up to bounded entropy slack.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Commutativity follows from symmetry of meet on modality maps and symmetric entropy max.
Idempotence holds because no new entropy is introduced.
Associativity may shift entropy by at most </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{merge}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, yielding equivalence up to tolerance, not strict equality.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Consequence.}
PlenumHub merge forms a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{bounded semilattice structure with entropy as its metric laxifier}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Media-Quine Closure as a Reflective Subcategory}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Closure Functor]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> complete missing modalities using only </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{permitted} transducers and bounded entropy synthesis.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Closed Sphere Subcategory]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Cls}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the full subcategory of spheres satisfying </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a Reflector]
The functor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is left adjoint to the inclusion </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>i:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Cls}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\hookrightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
Q </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dashv</span></span><span> i
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
and thus </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Cls}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{reflective subcategory}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
For every </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sph}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Cls}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, any morphism
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> factors uniquely through </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> because:
(1) </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> only adds modalities, never invalidates existing ones,
(2) closure is idempotent, and
(3) </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> already contains all required modalities.
This satisfies the universal property of reflection.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Implication.}
Semantic completion is not ad-hoc: it is the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{initial closed sphere} reachable from </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> by admissible transducers.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Equivariance as a Natural Transformation Law}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the symmetry group of meaning-preserving transformations (renaming, refactoring, paraphrase preserving invariants, basis change).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Equivariant Rule]
A rule </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{equivariant} if:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
r(g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) = g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> r(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> G.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Equivariant Closure Under Composition]
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are equivariant, then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is equivariant.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1)(g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)
= r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2(r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1(g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>))
= r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2(g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>))
= g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2(r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>))
= g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> (r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1)(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Consequence.}  
SpherePOP evolution preserves latent world symmetries—an algebraic guarantee absent from standard embedding or diffusion-based representation systems.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Summary of Categorical Guarantees}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

