6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Semantic Entropy Formalism}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:semantic-entropy}

This section formalizes the quantitative underpinnings of semantic entropy used throughout the PlenumHub substrate. We define the entropy measure on spheres, state entropy invariants, prove basic monotonicity results for rule composition, and derive bounds for merges and mediated reconciliation. Where appropriate we relate the formalism to classical information-theoretic results (Shannon, Kullback–Leibler) and to Landauer's principle to ground the thermodynamic intuition.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Definitions and desiderata}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Semantic sphere]
Recall a sphere is </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>=(I,T,M,E,S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denotes its semantic entropy. For each modality </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M(k)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denotes the modality value; write </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> when clear from context.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Modal divergence]
For a modality </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with content space </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> we suppose an embedding or representation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> that associates content with a probability distribution over relevant semantic primitives. Define a modality-local divergence between two contents </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k(v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2) := </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{KL}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k(v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k(v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Semantic entropy]
