</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Entropy invariants and long-term bounds}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[System entropy]
For a closed set of spheres </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{S}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (e.g., a repository snapshot), define system entropy
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{S}) := </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{S}} E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>')</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{MI}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a chosen set of cross-sphere edges (e.g., derived-by relations) and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> weights inter-sphere entanglement.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Bounded-growth under budgeted operations]
If every applied rule in the system carries a nonnegative budget and every merge consumes crystal stake that enforces </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then over any finite sequence of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> operations the system entropy satisfies
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{S}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+m}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{S}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(i)} + m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(i)}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are the budgets of the applied rules.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Additivity of budgets across rule applications and the per-merge slack </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> produce the bound by summation.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

The theorem yields a practical governance lever: by constraining per-operation budgets and maximum merge slack, operators can upper-bound long-term semantic disorder growth.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Thermodynamic grounding and Landauer analogy}

Semantic transformations that reduce uncertainty (e.g., specification, proof elaboration) are logically irreversible in the sense of information erasure at the representation level. Invoking Landauer's principle heuristically, there is a minimal physical cost to decrease Shannon entropy in the representation substrate. While PlenumHub does not mandate physical energy accounting, the analogy motivates the crystal economics: crystals are consumed when making irreversible semantic spends (e.g., authoritative merges, closure) to internalize a friction-like cost that discourages gratuitous entropy-reducing operations.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Summary and practical measures}

The semantic entropy formalism provides:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a principled measure </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> combining local uncertainty and cross-modal entanglement;
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> local accounting (entropy budgets </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) for typed rules and additive composition under rule-chains;
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a guarded merge mechanism whose admissibility is decidable from </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and a global slack </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>;
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> an optimization formulation for mediated reconciliation amenable to heuristic solvers.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

These components support the substrate's guarantees: bounded semantic drift, coherent merges, and auditable provenance of entropy-affecting operations.

