This section analyses the computational complexity of the SpherePOP primitives (pop, merge, closure) and describes approximation and distributed algorithms appropriate for practical implementations. We first state a cost model, then analyze worst-case, average-case, and amortized bounds; finally we propose heuristics and parallel strategies for scalability.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Cost model}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:cost-model}

We adopt a unit-cost model refined by modality-specific costs. Let:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the number of modalities present in a sphere (size of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>),
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the size of stored content for a sphere (sum of payload lengths),
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the length of a rule chain (number of rule applications),
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(k)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the cost to synthesize modality </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (e.g., run TTS, ASR, or an embedding model),
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{med}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the worst-case search cost of a mediation procedure,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{pop}}(r)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the cost of executing an individual rule </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (including any model call it invokes).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Costs are measured in time (wall-clock) and space (memory). Where a cost depends on content size </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, we indicate this explicitly, e.g., </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(k, s)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Pop (rule chain execution)}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:complexity-pop}

A pop operation applies a rule chain </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R = r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>; r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>r}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to an input sphere </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> producing </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Pop time complexity]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the number of rules in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The time complexity of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{pop}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>, R)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{pop}}(R) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>r} C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{pop}}(r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Hence </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{pop}}(R) = O(n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>r </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{pop}}(r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}[Sketch]
Each rule application is (by definition) performed sequentially and the cost of each is </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{pop}}(r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>; there is no asymptotically cheaper aggregate operation unless rules are commuted or fused by an optimizer (see Section~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:optimizations}).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Amortized and cached execution.}
When rule results are memoized keyed by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>, R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\suffix</span></span><span>})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> or when deterministic transducers have cacheable outputs, amortized cost per rule can be reduced. For frequently repeated subchains an LRU cache yields expected speedups; amortized cost becomes </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O(1)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> per rule in the ideal cached regime.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Merge complexity}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:complexity-merge}

Merging two spheres </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> has two phases:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Admissibility test:} compute candidate merged sphere </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and check entropy bound. This requires (a) combining modality maps and (b) computing or estimating </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Mediation search (if admissibility fails):} search for rewrite chains </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a, R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> producing </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that the merge is admissible.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Merge decision complexity]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a,m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the numbers of modalities in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a,s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be content sizes. Then:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{merge-decision}} = O(m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a + m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b + C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>E(s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a,s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b))
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the cost to compute or estimate semantic entropy over the union of modalities.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Mediation complexity (worst case).}
Mediation reduces to searching for sequences of rewrites </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a, R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> from potentially large spaces. In general this search is combinatorial and — in the absence of domain constraints or heuristics — may be </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{NP-hard}. Concretely:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Mediation hardness]
General mediation (finding </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a, R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> minimizing </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d(R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a), R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b))</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> subject to entropy bounds) is NP-hard, via reduction from constrained edit-distance / graph alignment problems.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Practical tractable regimes.}
To obtain tractability we recommend:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> restricting rule grammars (bounded-depth, bounded-branching),
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> limiting mediation search depth </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (yielding exponential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O(b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>D)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> but bounded),
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> accepting approximate merges using greedy or beam search (see next subsection).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Approximation algorithms for mediation and merge}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:approx-algos}

We propose three practical approaches with provable approximation characteristics in restricted settings.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Greedy heuristic}
Iteratively apply local rewrites that most reduce the entropy gap until the entropy bound is satisfied or a budget is exhausted. Empirically effective and runs in polynomial time, but may be suboptimal.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Beam search (bounded branching)}
Maintain a beam of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> candidate rewrite pairs </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a,R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Each step expands the beam by applying one additional rule to one side. Complexity per step is </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O(B </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> b </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{pop}})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is branching factor. Beam depth </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> yields </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O(B b D C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{pop}})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Beam search trades runtime for higher-quality merges.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{A* search with entropy-aware cost}
Define the state space as pairs </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a',</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b')</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and cost
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
g((</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a',</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b')) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{runtime cost so far},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
h((</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a',</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b')) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>0, E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a'</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b') - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>(E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a'),E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b'))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Under admissible heuristic </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, A* finds optimal mediation with respect to a chosen cost metric; runtime depends on heuristic quality and is exponential in worst case.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Approximation guarantees}
In restricted rule grammars where each rule reduces a monotone potential function by at least </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, greedy and beam search achieve an </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O(1)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-approximation to the optimal number of rule steps. Formal bounds depend on the potential function and require problem-specific analyses.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Closure (Media-Quine) complexity}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:complexity-closure}

Closure must ensure all required modalities are present, synthesizing missing ones via permitted transducers. If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the number of missing modalities and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the cost to synthesize a single modality, then:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>Q = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{missing}} C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(k) = O(m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>}).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Note that many transducers are expensive (e.g., TTS, ASR, synthesis); closure is therefore typically the dominant latency source in the runtime profile.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Parallel and distributed execution}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:parallel-dist}

SpherePOP operations admit parallelism at multiple levels:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Intra-pop parallelism}
If rule chain </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> decomposes into independent subchains operating on disjoint modality subsets, these can be executed concurrently. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the number of independent partitions; then ideal speedup is up to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (Amdahl's law applies).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Parallel merges}
Merges over disjoint sphere sets can be processed in parallel. For a set of pairs to be merged, throughput scales linearly with available workers until shared resources (CRDT stores, disk I/O, crystal accounting) throttle throughput.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Distributed mediation}
Mediation search can be distributed using map-reduce or master-worker patterns:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Workers explore disjoint rewrite subspaces or beam candidates,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Master aggregates best candidates and decides acceptance.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}
This design reduces wall time at the expense of increased total CPU-hours and coordination overhead.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Consensus and crystal accounting}
Quorate merges require a consensus step to debit crystal accounts atomically. Use scalable consensus primitives:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> optimistic batching with conflict detection and retry for light workloads,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Paxos/Raft-style consensus for strong consistency on small committees,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> probabilistic quorum (gossip-based) for high-throughput, eventual consistency scenarios.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Complexity summary and practical guidance}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Pop:} linear in rule-chain length; amenable to caching and JIT optimizations.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Merge-decision:} polynomial with respect to modality count and content size; dominated by entropy computation and content synthesis.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Mediation:} combinatorial in the worst case; use heuristics (beam, greedy, A*) for production systems.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Closure:} linear in number of missing modalities times synthesis cost; transducer performance matters.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Parallelism:} significant opportunities exist — exploit modality partitioning and distributed mediation — but be careful with crystal-accounting contention and provenance linearizability.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Optimizations and engineering considerations}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:optimizations}

We recommend the following to make the substrate practical at scale:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Memoization / content-addressed caches:} Key caches on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>, R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\suffix</span></span><span>})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> pairs to amortize repeated transformations.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Incremental entropy estimation:} Maintain incremental statistics for entropy so that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> updates on small edits are </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O(1)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> amortized.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Rule specialization and fusion:} Statistically hot rule sequences can be fused into single optimized transducers.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Lazy closure:} Delay expensive transductions until a modality is required by a consumer (on-demand synthesis), combined with a freshness budget.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Approximate merges:} Prefer fast approximate merges with post-hoc verification when strong correctness is not immediately required.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

These optimizations, together with the algorithms described above, provide a tractable engineering path from the abstract SpherePOP calculus to a performant implementation suitable for real-world collaborative knowledge workflows.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
