.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% Section: Monoidal Extension, Crystal Functor, Entropy-Lax Structure</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% Insert after the Merge Contractibility section</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Monoidal Extension, Crystal Valuation, and Entropy-Lax Structure}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:monoidal-crystal-entropy}

This section upgrades the fibrational merge picture to a symmetric monoidal setting, introduces a {</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\em</span></span><span> crystal valuation} assigning nonnegative real costs to spheres and merges, and proves an entropy-lax monoidality property: tensoring of spheres is compatible with cocartesian transport and is subadditive with respect to semantic entropy.

Throughout we work in the quasicategorical language of Section 1: </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>p:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a coCartesian fibration with groupoid fibers, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is endowed with a symmetric monoidal structure </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{I})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> modelling parallel composition of histories (e.g., independent branches, spatial adjacency). We denote fiber functors by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F(h)=p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}(h)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Symmetric Monoidal History Fibration}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Symmetric monoidal history fibration]
A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{symmetric monoidal history fibration} is a coCartesian fibration
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
p : (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
together with a unital, associative, symmetric lift datum such that:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> for every pair of base objects </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the external tensor restricts to a functor on fibers
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> : F(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> F(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\longrightarrow</span></span><span> F(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}} h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2),
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
  functorial in cocartesian base-change maps; and
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> cocartesian edges are stable under tensoring with identities: if </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e:x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> x'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-cocartesian over </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>:h</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> h'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then for any </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> F(k)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the edge
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
  e</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{id}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>y : x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> y </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> x'</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> y
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
  is </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-cocartesian over </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{id}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k : h</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> h'</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (and symmetrically on the right).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

This definition is the fibrational analogue of a symmetric monoidal functor of quasicategories (see HTT/HA style constructions). It encodes the claim that the tensor on histories lifts coherently to a tensor on semantic states and that cocartesian transport commutes (up to coherent homotopy) with tensoring.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Monoidal lift of merges]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>p:(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be a symmetric monoidal history fibration with groupoid fibers. Given merge spans
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i = (h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i,1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xleftarrow</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i} h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i,0}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i} h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i,2}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> (i=1,2)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
and pairs </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i,1},x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i,2})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> F(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i,1})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> F(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i,2})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the tensored span
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2
=
(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1,1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{2,1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leftarrow</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1,0}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{2,0}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1,2}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{2,2})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
realizes a canonical merge for </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1,1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{2,1},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1,2}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{2,2})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> obtained by tensoring cocartesian lifts of each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Moreover the space of such tensored cocartesian lifts is contractible.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Take cocartesian realizers </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i,1},x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i,2})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as in Section 3. By the stability-of-cocartesian-under-tensor condition the external tensor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is cocartesian over the tensor of base arrows. Contractibility follows because each factor space is contractible and the tensor of contractible Kan complexes is contractible (products preserve contractibility).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Crystal Valuation and Its Functoriality}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Crystal valuation]
A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{crystal valuation} is a map of symmetric monoidal quasicategories (lax or strong depending on context)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span> : (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\longrightarrow</span></span><span> (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0}, +, 0)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where the target is regarded as a discrete symmetric monoidal </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-category (or simply a commutative monoid) with addition as tensor. Concretely, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> assigns to each sphere </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> a nonnegative real number </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (the crystal cost), and to each cocartesian morphism </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> a nonnegative increment </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(e)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfying natural composition laws.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{remark}
We permit </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to be </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{lax monoidal} (there is a canonical comparison map </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) because some physical or economic subtleties (discounts, economies of scale) may make exact additivity undesirable. In practice one wants </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to be </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{additive} on independent modalities and only subadditive under entropic synergies.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{remark}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Functoriality of crystals under cocartesian transport]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{thm:crystal-functoriality}
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be a symmetric monoidal history fibration and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> a crystal valuation which is (lax) monoidal and preserves equivalences in fibers. Then for any cocartesian edge </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> over </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>:h</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> h'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> we have
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>') </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(e),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
and the assignment </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mapsto</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>))</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is constant along equivalences in the fiber and functorial in base-change: if </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f:k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is any map in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>*</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denotes reindexing, then
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>*(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{(up to the canonical comparison isomorphism).}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Cocartesian transport is functorial and composes; since </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> respects composition of cocartesian morphisms and the monoidal structure (laxly), the increment associated to a composite cocartesian path is the sum of its increments. Preservation along base-change follows from naturality of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as a (lax) symmetric monoidal functor and uniqueness-of-cocartesian-lifts up to contractible choice.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Entropy as a First-Class Quantity and Entropy-Lax Monoidality}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote semantic entropy (the map sending each sphere to its entropy value). We assume:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is invariant under equivalence in fibers,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> increases under cocartesian morphisms by the rule </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>')</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is an entropy increment associated to the edge </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Entropy-lax symmetric monoidal structure]
The pair </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E},E)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{entropy-lax symmetric monoidal} if there exists a coherent family of comparison inequalities
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) + E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a bounded synergy term which is natural in cocartesian transport and satisfies the coherence axioms of a lax monoidal structure (associativity and symmetry constraints commute with the inequalities).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Entropy subadditivity under tensor and cocartesian stability]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{thm:entropy-subadditive}
Under the standing hypotheses (symmetric monoidal history fibration, groupoid fibers, crystal valuation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> lax monoidal, and entropy map </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as above), the following hold:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> (Subadditivity) For all </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> F(h)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> F(k)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
  E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>) + E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>},
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
  where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a uniform bound on synergy terms for the class of spheres considered.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> (Cocartesian transport invariance modulo increments) If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are cocartesian, then
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
  E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>') </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>f + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>' ,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
  where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a bounded correction arising from nontrivial interaction of cocartesian increments.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> (Entropy budget for merges) If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a valid merge in the sense of Section~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:typed-calculus}, then
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
  E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\oplus</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a),E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{merge}},
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
  and tensoring both sides with some </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> preserves the inequality up to the uniform synergy bound.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
(1) follows from the definition of entropy-lax monoidality and taking </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>}=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sup</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. (2) follows by combining the cocartesian increment inequalities on each factor and applying the subadditivity inequality to the tensored endpoints; any interaction term accumulating from the two increments is absorbed into </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. (3) is the tensor-stability statement: since merges are realized by cocartesian lifts (Section~3) and cocartesian transport interacts with tensoring (by the symmetric monoidal fibration hypothesis), the merge entropy bound transports to the tensor product with controlled correction.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Corollaries and Practical Interpretation}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}[Crystal-entropy alignment]
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is chosen proportional to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (or more generally </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> c</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for some </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>), then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> inherits the entropy-lax monoidal inequalities and can be used interchangeably as an economic proxy for entropic cost in mediation/quorum algorithms.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}[Merge cost functoriality]
The crystal cost of a canonical merge obtained by tensoring merges equals the sum of crystal costs of the component merges up to the lax comparison map; hence microeconomic accounting of crystal expenditures is coherent with homotopy-coherent merging.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

